\section{Move an image without rewriting its instructions}

Position-independent code (PIC) keeps instruction bytes usable at different addresses. It does not require every address in the image to be relative. A writable table can contain absolute addresses, provided the instructions reach its entries without embedding those addresses. Levine separates invariant code from process-specific data to explain why different processes can map the same code pages without each making a relocated copy \cite[chapter~8]{levine2000linkers}. The load bias can vary between processes, but it shifts all the loadable segments of one image by the same amount. The distance from a code location to a fixed data location in the image is therefore unchanged.

Levine's historical examples solve the same problem in different ways. TSS/360 passed a routine's data address along with its code address. AIX can keep a table-of-contents address in a designated register across calls according to its own conventions. The i386 ELF example obtains a program counter with a call and pop, then forms the GOT base. None of these is the small-model AMD64 instruction sequence: AMD64 can address the Global Offset Table (GOT) directly relative to the instruction pointer, without reserving a GOT base register for each function. The AMD64 ABI supplement's \S3.5 explains how larger addressability requirements change the instruction sequences.

\section{Distance and binding are separate questions}

An assembler can encode an instruction aimed at a symbol whose distance is not yet known. It leaves a relocation for the static linker to complete once the output layout is known. When the target is an ordinary local or hidden symbol in the same image, a direct RIP-relative reference can work if the target is in range. A default-visible definition in a shared library can be preemptible: some references can bind to a different definition selected by the loader even when the link editor sees a definition in this library. In that case a direct reference to the local copy would implement the wrong binding. A reference through a GOT slot separates the fixed distance to the slot from the choice of what runtime address to store there.

A related split applies to many calls across images: the local Procedure Linkage Table (PLT) entry is reachable at a fixed distance, but its underlying slot can point to a routine in a separately based image \cite[chapter~10]{levine2000linkers}. Some code uses indirect calls through GOT slots without an ordinary PLT call. For either data or functions, the table entry is indirection, not a way to make the ultimate target address itself invariant.

