\section{Choose a reachable representation}

Consider a simplified code generator for small-model x86-64. Each displacement is measured from the end of the instruction that uses it. The emitter picks an addressing form; the link editor checks its actual displacement once it knows the final image layout.

\begin{verbatim}
if definition_is_fixed_within_image:
    target = symbol_vaddr       # no runtime choice of definition
else:
    target = got_slot_vaddr     # only the slot's location is fixed
disp = target - next_instruction_vaddr
require -2^31 <= disp < 2^31    # avoid silent signed truncation
encode disp; if using a slot, load its value before use
\end{verbatim}

At next-instruction address \texttt{0x1017}, direct access to local data at \texttt{0x3020} needs \texttt{0x2009}. Access to a GOT slot at \texttt{0x3fd0} from a different instruction ending at \texttt{0x1020} needs \texttt{0x2fb0}. With bias \texttt{0x50000000} the slot's runtime address is \texttt{0x50003fd0}; it may contain \texttt{0x60004000}, an address in a provider image. If only the consumer moves up by \texttt{0x10000}, repeat the calculation: \texttt{0x50013fd0} minus \texttt{0x50011020} still gives \texttt{0x2fb0}. The slot moves but its resolved value does not. The invariant is that the displacement depends only on the two positions within the consumer.

Test exact positive and negative range endpoints: \texttt{0x7fffffff} and negative \texttt{0x80000000} fit, but positive \texttt{0x80000000} cannot be encoded as a signed 32-bit displacement. One must change the chosen representation or layout rather than write the truncated bits. Toolchains offer code models to express such placement assumptions; PIC by itself is not a guarantee of unlimited reach.

An ELF relocation formula may express the same distance from a different origin. If the four-byte relocation field starts at $P$ and ends the instruction, then next RIP is $P+4$. The \texttt{PC32} calculation $S+A-P$ with addend $A=-4$ expresses exactly that distance when $S$ is the target.

