\section{Inspect both stages of address formation}

With an AMD64 GNU toolchain, put \texttt{static volatile int n = 7;} and \texttt{extern int remote;} in \texttt{access.c}. Define \texttt{int sum(void)\{return n + remote;\}} and compile with \texttt{cc -O2 -fPIC -c access.c}. In \texttt{objdump -dr access.o}, find a direct PC-relative access to local storage and a GOT-relative relocation for \texttt{remote}. In the object file the relocation refers to a not-yet-final displacement; it does not give the address of \texttt{remote}. Link with \texttt{cc -shared access.o -o libaccess.so} and inspect \texttt{readelf -rW libaccess.so}. Expect a dynamic relocation for the imported address, not for the resolved code displacement. This fixture is inspected rather than loaded, so it needs no provider for \texttt{remote}. When binding is known to be local and range permits, linkers can sometimes relax a GOT load to direct addressing. The key test remains whether executable bytes are independent of the runtime bias and whether the output still implements the promised binding.
